Repository (Repo) – A container for your project’s files, documentation, and revision history. It can be public or private and supports collaboration through Git.

Branch – A parallel version of the repository used to develop features or fix bugs without affecting the main branch. Common types include main, feature branch, and topic branch.

Commit – A snapshot of changes in the repository, identified by a unique SHA (commit ID). Each commit includes a commit message describing the change.

Clone – A local copy of a remote repository, enabling offline work while maintaining a connection for syncing changes.

Pull, Push, Fetch –

Pull: Fetches and merges changes from remote to local.

Push: Sends local commits to the remote repository.

Fetch: Downloads changes without merging.

Pull Request (PR) – A request to merge changes from one branch into another. PRs include discussion threads, code reviews, and status checks before merging.

Merge & Merge Conflict –

Merge: Combines changes from one branch into another.

Merge Conflict: Occurs when changes in two branches affect the same lines of code and must be resolved manually.

Fork – A personal copy of another user’s repository, allowing independent changes while keeping a link to the original (upstream) repo.
